1. Understand the source data
Start with one known Stage IV day — July 15, 2026 — and confirm exactly what the Texas parquet contains: grib_id, rainfall field, row count, rainfall range, etc.

2. Reconstruct the authoritative HRAP rainfall surface
Join the parquet to the full Texas HRAP grid by grib_id. This is still the real ~4-km Stage IV data. No invented resolution yet.

3. Reproduce your existing smoothing method
Use the method you already know looks good:
HRAP polygons → EPSG:3857 native raster → 10× bilinear upsample → Gaussian radius 2.
Your current mapper already follows essentially this sequence.

4. Separate analysis resolution from display resolution
The HRAP/parquet remains authoritative for calculations. The ~400-m-ish smoothed raster is explicitly a display surface, not higher-resolution rainfall observations.

5. Create one statewide COG
Instead of converting the smooth raster to millions of x/y/value dataframe rows for ggplot, write the SpatRaster directly to a Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF. That is the big architectural simplification.

6. QA the COG locally
Open it in QGIS and verify:
no visible HRAP blocks,
rainfall cores look reasonable,
zero/rain boundaries behave well,
no edge artifacts,
no strange values introduced by smoothing.

7. Learn how the COG behaves as a web asset
Put that one July 15 COG in S3, expose it through CloudFront, and verify byte-range requests just like we did with PMTiles.

8. Render rainfall in MapLibre
Add the COG to the existing map-poc. Apply the rainfall palette, transparency, opacity, and layer ordering in the browser rather than baking those things into a PNG.

9. Test interaction and devices
Desktop and phone. Zoom way in. Pan around Texas. Check whether the smooth raster still looks convincing at the zoom levels we actually care about.

10. Only then design production
Once the July 15 surface looks excellent, determine how the production pipeline will create Texas-wide:
daily
7-day
30-day
YTD